  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 AM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe weather until the end of the week remains pretty stagnant.\nWe rare gentle northwest to west flow across the state. This\nwill keep us a few degrees cooler than past days, BUT still run\nquite a bit ABOVE AVERAGE for early October. We will begin to\nsee the first influences of deepening low pressure over the\nwestern part of North America Friday. We will likely see some\nmid and high level clouds increasing but stay dry. Winds will\nramp up as the overall pattern shifts down toward the Pacific\nNorthwest. \n\nStarting Saturday and continuing through the middle of next\nweek, we will see MUCH COOLER conditions with the potential for\nrain and snow during that period. The low drops over Oregon and\nWashington and begins to closed off. We will drop into the mid\n60s to lows 70s in the valleys as clouds and precipitation\nincrease. One of the potential forecast \"solutions\" since last\nweek in the GFS and ECMWF ensembles is taking the low quickly \nto offshore of California and we end up initially with split \nflow over Idaho. At least for today, that appears the main\nforecast idea across most of the operational and AI versions of\nthose models. While still one of the ideas with the ensemble\nforecasts, they still show the previous idea of the low not\ndropping that far south. With the current trend toward the more\nsouthern area of low pressure, this has the potential of drawing\nin the remnants of Rachel and producing a bit of precipitation\nacross the southeast corner of the state. This morning, the\nBlend of Models does show that with 1\"+ amounts. We will see\nthat change? Likely since we are still a few days out and we see\nbig swings sometimes with the models in a pattern like this. The\nkeep in mind with some of the uncertainty, the overall idea\nstill remains the same...cooler and wet conditions. There is\nstill hint we will see snow across higher elevations especially\nthe central mountains and eastern highlands. The chance is still\nvery, very low for any snow across lower valleys, but that is\ntrending closer to zero at the moment.",
